# Processes and CPU Scheduling: Who Gets the Processor Next

> A program on disk is inert; a process is that program brought to
> life, competing for the CPU. The scheduler decides who runs next.

## 1. From Program to Process

A **program** is a passive file on disk; a **process** is that
program *in execution* — code loaded into memory, plus bookkeeping.

@diagram tree id="process-states" layout="tree" aria="Process state machine with transition triggers from new to terminated"
  "New (OS admits it)" -> "Ready"
  "Ready (dispatcher picks it)" -> "Running"
  "Running (timer expires)" -> "Ready"
  "Running (requests I/O)" -> "Waiting"
  "Running (finishes or is killed)" -> "Terminated"
  "Waiting (event completes)" -> "Ready"
@/diagram

[definition]
**Preemption** is taking the CPU away from a running process before
it finishes — its time slice ran out or a more urgent one arrived.
[/definition]

## 2. The PCB and the Cost of Switching

The OS keeps one **Process Control Block (PCB)** per process — the
process's identity card in kernel memory:

| PCB field | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| Process ID and state | Unique number; ready, running, waiting, ... |
| Program counter + registers | Where execution is; saved CPU contents |
| Scheduling info | Priority, quantum used, queue links |
| Memory and I/O info | Page tables; open files, devices |

A **context switch** saves the current process's state into its PCB
and loads the next process's. It is pure overhead — no user work
happens during a switch — costing microseconds.

## 3. Processes vs Threads

A **thread** is a unit of execution *inside* a process; sibling
threads share its address space, code, and open files.

| Property | Process | Thread |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Address space | Own, isolated | Shared with siblings |
| Creation cost | Heavy — new memory maps | Light — stack plus registers |
| Communication | Pipes and messages, slower | Shared variables, needs locks |
| Crash impact | One death rarely harms others | One bad thread corrupts all |

## 4. What a Scheduler Tries to Optimize

The unit scheduled is the **CPU burst** — a stretch of pure
computation between two I/O requests. Interactive systems care most
about **response time**; batch systems care about throughput.

| Criterion | Meaning | Formula or idea |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CPU utilization | Keep the CPU busy | Percent of time busy |
| Throughput | Work completed | Processes finished per unit time |
| Turnaround time | Total time in the system | Completion − arrival |
| Waiting time | Time in the ready queue | Turnaround − CPU burst |
| Response time | React quickly to input | First response − arrival |

## 5. A Worked Example: Three Processes

| Process | Arrival | Burst |
| --- | --- | --- |
| P1 | 0 | 24 |
| P2 | 0 | 3 |
| P3 | 0 | 3 |

**FCFS** (first-come, first-served) runs them in arrival order —
P1 (0–24), P2 (24–27), P3 (27–30); waits 0, 24, 27, average 17 ms.
The long P1 drags the short jobs behind it: the **convoy effect**.

**SJF** (shortest job first) runs P2 (0–3), P3 (3–6), P1 (6–30);
waits 0, 3, 6 — average 3 ms, provably optimal for a fixed batch.

[warning]
SJF's optimality has two prices: it must know future bursts (in
practice estimated from past ones), and long jobs can **starve**
while shorter jobs keep arriving.
[/warning]

**Round Robin** with quantum q = 4 ms: P1 runs 0–4, P2 finishes
4–7, P3 finishes 7–10, P1 runs 10–30; waits 6, 4, 7, average ≈
5.7 ms — a bit worse than SJF, but every process touches the CPU
quickly, which interactive users feel.

@chart bar title="Average waiting time in the worked example (ms)"
labels:
FCFS
SJF
Round Robin

data:
17
3
5.7
@/chart

## 6. The Algorithm Zoo

**SRTF** (shortest remaining time first) is preemptive SJF: switch
whenever an arrival needs less time than the running job has left.
**Priority scheduling** runs the highest-priority ready process, but
can starve low-priority ones unless **aging** raises the priority of
long-waiting processes.

| Algorithm | Preemptive? | Starvation? | Signature property |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| FCFS | No | No | Simple FIFO; convoy effect |
| SJF | No | Yes | Optimal average waiting time |
| SRTF | Yes | Yes | Preemptive SJF, better response |
| Round Robin | Yes | No | Fair slices; quantum trade-off |
| Priority | Either | Yes | Aging is the standard fix |

[tip]
Round Robin's quantum is a trade-off: too large and it degenerates
into FCFS; too small and context-switch overhead dominates.
[/tip]

## 7. Concurrency vs Parallelism

**Concurrency** is structure: many processes make progress by
interleaving on one core. **Parallelism** is simultaneity: several
cores truly execute at once. The **dispatcher** hands the CPU to the
chosen process — context switch, switch to user mode, jump.

## Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| Context switch | Save one PCB, load another; pure overhead |
| Turnaround / waiting | Completion − arrival / turnaround − burst |
| Convoy effect | Short jobs stuck behind one long job (FCFS) |
| Concurrency vs parallelism | Interleaved progress vs simultaneous execution |
